// Code signing. Builds are signed ad hoc by default (as CI does). To sign your own builds with a stable
// identity, create Local.xcconfig next to this file (it is gitignored), for example:
//
//   CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Developer ID Application
//   DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = ABCDE12345
//
// macOS ties privacy permissions (such as access to Documents) to the app's signature. Ad-hoc builds
// get a new identity on every build, so macOS asks again each time; a stable identity keeps the answer.
//
// Lock-screen alerts for Coincide Remote need the Mac app signed for the iCloud container
// iCloud.com.apoorvdarshan.coincide, with a provisioning profile that includes it:
//
//   COINCIDE_MAC_ENTITLEMENTS = Coincide/CoincideCloudKit.entitlements
//   COINCIDE_MAC_PROFILE = <name of a Developer ID provisioning profile with that container>
//
// Without them Coincide never touches CloudKit, and alerts stay on the local network.
//
// Passkeys and security keys in the browser panel need Apple's managed entitlement
// com.apple.developer.web-browser.public-key-credential. Without it, a page's passkey request fails and the panel
// suggests signing in another way. Once Apple has granted it to the team:
//
//   1. Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles › Identifiers › com.apoorvdarshan.coincide: turn on the Web Browser
//      Public Key Credential capability (it appears there only after approval) and save.
//   2. Profiles: create a Developer ID provisioning profile for that App ID (or edit and regenerate the existing one,
//      since a profile made before the capability doesn't carry it), download it and open it to install it.
//   3. Here in Local.xcconfig, sign with that profile and an entitlements file that has the key:
//
//        COINCIDE_MAC_ENTITLEMENTS = Coincide/Passkeys.entitlements
//        COINCIDE_MAC_PROFILE = <name of that Developer ID provisioning profile>
//
//      With lock-screen alerts too, use Coincide/CoincideCloudKitPasskeys.entitlements and a profile with both.
//   4. Run xcodegen generate and build. `codesign -d --entitlements - Coincide.app` should list the key, and the panel
//      no longer shows "Passkeys aren't available" (it checks the running app's signature).
//
// Don't add the key without such a profile: macOS refuses to launch an app claiming a managed entitlement its
// profile doesn't grant.
//
// Published releases (docs/releasing.md) come from scripts/release/build.sh, which overrides these settings on the
// command line: Developer ID, the hardened runtime and Coincide/Coincide.entitlements (microphone and Apple Events, which
// the hardened runtime otherwise blocks), with no provisioning profile. The entitlements files above carry the same two
// keys, so a hardened build signed with any of them keeps dictation and Computer Use.
//
// COINCIDE_MAC_ENTITLEMENTS and COINCIDE_MAC_PROFILE apply to Release builds only. Debug builds have their own bundle
// ID (com.apoorvdarshan.coincide.debug, see COINCIDE_BUNDLE_ID in project.yml), which a profile for
// com.apoorvdarshan.coincide doesn't cover, so they never sign for iCloud or passkeys.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"

// The build number (CFBundleVersion) of every target: the number of commits in HEAD's history, which
// scripts/build-number.sh writes to the gitignored BuildNumber.xcconfig whenever xcodegen generates the project.
CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION = 1
#include? "BuildNumber.xcconfig"
